<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmad ibn Idris</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1760">1760</a>–<a href="/years/1837">1837</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ahmad ibn Idris al-Fasi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmad ibn Idris al-Fasi was a Moroccan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> teacher whose reformist teaching stressed the direct study of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a>. He taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and finally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabya">Sabya</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asir">Asir</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, where he died. His students founded several influential Sufi orders, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senussi-order">Sanusiyya</a>, and his great-grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-ali-al-idrisi">Muhammad ibn Ali al-Idrisi</a> founded the Idrisid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Asir.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ahmad ibn Idris was born in 1760 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> and studied at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-al-qarawiyyin">University of al-Qarawiyyin</a>. He arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 1799, and there he had his widest influence, drawing students from every part of the Islamic world. In 1828 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, long a great centre of Muslim learning, and he was active as a scholar, jurist and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and Yemen. He died in 1837 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabya">Sabya</a>, then part of Yemen and now of Saudi Arabia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His teaching centred on the moral and spiritual education of the individual Muslim: piety, prayer, religious learning, above all the Prophetic traditions, and close imitation of Muhammad's example. He sought to revive the Sunnah and to purify <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> of what he judged to be bid'ah and shirk, sent students to revive the Sunnah in other lands, and called for a renewal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijtihad">ijtihad</a>.</p><p>He rejected following any one school of jurisprudence, for three reasons: the need to follow the Prophetic traditions, the wish to reduce divisions among Muslims, and mercy, since he held that where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and Sunnah were silent the silence was God's deliberate mercy, which no one should fill. His order, the Idrisiyya or Tariqa Muhammadiyya, was less an organised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> order than a method of beliefs and practices meant, in its members' view, to bind the disciple directly to Muhammad with little mediation by any other human authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His followers carried his teaching across the Muslim world. Mohammed Uthman al-Mirghani al-Khatim founded the Khatmiyya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, Abd al-Rahman Nurow spread the Tariqa Muhammadiyya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, Abu'l 'Abbas al-Dandarawi founded the Dandarawiyya, and Salih al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jafari-school">Ja'fari</a> edited his works and founded the Ja'fariyya. Muhammad Said al-Linggi's followers brought the path to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, and Hafiz Muhammad Amin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> introduced it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Sabya later became the capital of the Idrisid Emirate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asir">Asir</a>, founded by his descendant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-ali-al-idrisi">Muhammad ibn Ali al-Idrisi</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ahmad ibn Idris al-Fasi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Muhammad ibn Ali al-Idrisi, great-grandson of the Moroccan Sufi teacher <b>Ahmad ibn Idris</b>, settled at Sabya in the coastal Tihama of Asir.</blockquote><a href="/state/idrisid-emirate-of-asir">Idrisid Emirate of Asir</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fez, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.04,-5&amp;year=1810" aria-label="Fez on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4423 930 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ahmad ibn Idris"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4873 1200h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4873 1200h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.04,-5&amp;year=1810">What was here in 1810? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
